the k68 is the latest mechanical  keyboard from corsair and it's meant for  the slightly more budget-conscious pc  gamer it's one of the few mechanical  keyboards from a big manufacturer with a  retail price at or under a hundred bucks  [Music]  we previously reviewed Logitech sub $100  offering in the g4 13 so check that out  after this video but back to the K 68  it's essentially an iteration on the  Corsair strafe as it shares a very  similar design in price point but the K  68 makes key improvements keystrokes are  much quieter due to the rubber like  casing that lines of the mechanical  switches it doesn't affect the  functionality of the keyboard and the  linear non tactile Cherry MX red  mechanical switches still provide a  smooth and responsive feel note that  there aren't any options for other  switch types and the clumsy folk out  there like myself will be at ease with  the k68 as it's waterproof up to IP 32  standards this basically means that  spilled water that comes down on the  keyboard up to a 15 degree angle won't  damage the keyboard thanks to the rubber  casing underneath the key caps and there  are vents at the bottom to let out any  liquid that drops into it another  significant improvement is the addition  of dedicated media keys play pause stop  and volume control have their own  buttons and makes handling music apps  much easier than using alternate modes  on a function row but why would you  spend all this money on a keyboard  anyway well mechanical switches not only  make gaming more comfortable but the  responsiveness is an advantage in  competitive games especially if you come  from a standard squishy membrane  keyboard this was most apparent during  my time with battlefield 1 and Counter  Strike global Offensive split-second  reactions matter in these fast-paced  first-person shooters being able to hit  the ground prone or swap weapons at a  moment's notice can make all the  difference in firefights for battlefield  one constantly strafing left and right  is an important tactic and Counter  Strike and the bouncing responsiveness  of the keys allowed me to pull this off  with ease since mechanical switches have  a distinct actuation point well know  exactly when a key is being pressed and  key strokes will register without  failure I personally like the square  blocky design of the keyboard and  appreciate its simplicity and that  textured spacebar is pretty sweet  but if  you're looking for a bit more style the  k68 probably won't excite you it only  features red backlighting but you can  customize the lighting behavior in the  corsair q software although it's not the  most user-friendly peripheral software  out there the packaging also comes with  a detachable plastic palm rest which can  improve comfort but it's a bit curious  as to why the k68 doesn't come with a  key cap puller which is essential for  getting underneath the keys for cleaning  overall there's a lot to like about the  corsair k68 since it doesn't really do  anything wrong and iterates on a  previous keyboard that was already great  will you want to switch to the k68 if  you already have a mechanical keyboard  probably not hundred bucks is a hundred  bucks but it's more affordable than  other corsair keyboards like the case 70  inkay 95 it's still a big purchase  decision but if you want to jump to a  mechanical keyboard with cherry MX Reds  it doesn't get much better than the k68  I know you'll like this video so give us  a thumbs up and if you want more reviews  and discussions of gaming tech subscribe  to our channel here at GameSpot thanks  for watching  [Music]
 

